\section*{Question 4}

A particle moves in the \(x y\)-plane so that the position of the particle at any time \(t\) is given by
\[
x(t)=2 e^{3 t}+e^{-7 t} \text { and } y(t)=3 e^{3 t}-e^{-2 t} .
\]
\begin{itemize}
\item[(a)] Find the velocity vector for the particle in terms of \(t\), and find the speed of the particle at time \(t=0\).
\item[(b)] Find \(\frac{d y}{d x}\) in terms of \(t\), and find \(\lim _{t \rightarrow \infty} \frac{d y}{d x}\).
\item[(c)] Find each value \(t\) at which the line tangent to the path of the particle is horizontal, or explain why none exists.
\item[(d)] Find each value \(t\) at which the line tangent to the path of the particle is vertical, or explain why none exists.
\item[(a)]
\[
\begin{aligned}
& x^{\prime}(t)=6 e^{3 t}-7 e^{-7 t} \\
& y^{\prime}(t)=9 e^{3 t}+2 e^{-2 t} \\
& \text { Velocity vector is }<6 e^{3 t}-7 e^{-7 t}, 9 e^{3 t}+2 e^{-2 t}>
\end{aligned}
\]
\[
\begin{aligned}
\text { Speed } & =\sqrt{x^{\prime}(0)^{2}+y^{\prime}(0)^{2}}=\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+11^{2}} \\
& =\sqrt{122}
\end{aligned}
\]
\item[(b)] \(\frac{d y}{d x}=\frac{\frac{d y}{d t}}{\frac{d x}{d t}}=\frac{9 e^{3 t}+2 e^{-2 t}}{6 e^{3 t}-7 e^{-7 t}}\)
\[
\lim _{t \rightarrow \infty} \frac{d y}{d x}=\lim _{t \rightarrow \infty} \frac{9 e^{3 t}+2 e^{-2 t}}{6 e^{3 t}-7 e^{-7 t}}=\frac{9}{6}=\frac{3}{2}
\]
\item[(c)] Need \(y^{\prime}(t)=0\), but \(9 e^{3 t}+2 e^{-2 t}>0\) for all \(t\), so none exists.
\end{itemize}
\[
3:\left\{\begin{array}{l}
1: x^{\prime}(t) \\
1: y^{\prime}(t) \\
1: \text { speed }
\end{array}\right.
\]
\[
2:\left\{\begin{array}{l}
1: \frac{d y}{d x} \text { in terms of } t \\
1: \text { limit }
\end{array}\right.
\]
\[
2:\left\{\begin{array}{l}
1: \text { considers } y^{\prime}(t)=0 \\
1: \text { explains why none exists }
\end{array}\right.
\]
\[
2:\left\{\begin{array}{l}
1: \text { considers } x^{\prime}(t)=0 \\
1: \text { solution }
\end{array}\right.
\]
\begin{itemize}
\item[(d)] Need \(x^{\prime}(t)=0\) and \(y^{\prime}(t) \neq 0\).
\[
\begin{aligned}
& 6 e^{3 t}=7 e^{-7 t} \\
& e^{10 t}=\frac{7}{6} \\
& t=\frac{1}{10} \ln \left(\frac{7}{6}\right)
\end{aligned}
\]
\end{itemize}
